    "body": "## Problem\r\n\r\nWhen nested LSP folding ranges end on the same row, `foldexpr()` emits only the highest ending level. This can merge an adjacent outer range into the preceding one.\r\n\r\n## Reproduction\r\n\r\nThe pre-patch behavior below was reproduced on Neovim `master` at [`f583991553`](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/commit/f5839915537597ae047a0a9fd674bc377071f1b3).\r\n\r\nGiven a nine-line buffer, return these zero-based LSP folding ranges:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n{ startLine = 0, endLine = 5 } -- outer A\r\n{ startLine = 0, endLine = 1 } -- nested A1\r\n{ startLine = 2, endLine = 5 } -- nested A2\r\n{ startLine = 6, endLine = 8 } -- outer B\r\n{ startLine = 6, endLine = 7 } -- nested B1\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith `foldmethod=expr`, `foldexpr=v:lua.vim.lsp.foldexpr()`, and `foldminlines=0`, run `zM`. Before this patch, the fold starting at line 1 can extend through line 9 and absorb outer range B. After this patch, the closed outer folds remain separate at lines 1-6 and 7-9.\r\n\r\nRun this from the repository root after building Nvim. It injects ranges through the internal folding-range state for reproduction only; the functional test below provides the regression coverage.\r\n\r\n```sh\r\nVIMRUNTIME=\"$PWD/runtime\" ./build/bin/nvim --clean --headless -l - <<'LUA'\r\nlocal api = vim.api\r\napi.nvim_buf_set_lines(0, 0, -1, false, {\r\n  'one', 'two', 'three', 'four', 'five',\r\n  'six', 'seven', 'eight', 'nine',\r\n})\r\n\r\nrequire('vim.lsp._folding_range')\r\nlocal State = require('vim.lsp._capability').all.folding_range\r\nlocal state = State:new(api.nvim_get_current_buf())\r\nstate.client_state[1] = {\r\n  { startLine = 0, endLine = 5 },\r\n  { startLine = 0, endLine = 1 },\r\n  { startLine = 2, endLine = 5 },\r\n  { startLine = 6, endLine = 8 },\r\n  { startLine = 6, endLine = 7 },\r\n}\r\nstate:evaluate()\r\n\r\nvim.wo.foldmethod = 'expr'\r\nvim.wo.foldexpr = 'v:lua.vim.lsp.foldexpr()'\r\nvim.wo.foldminlines = 0\r\nvim._foldupdate(api.nvim_get_current_win(), 0, api.nvim_buf_line_count(0))\r\nvim.cmd('normal! zM')\r\n\r\nprint(vim.inspect({\r\n  vim.fn.foldclosed(1), vim.fn.foldclosedend(1),\r\n  vim.fn.foldclosed(7), vim.fn.foldclosedend(7),\r\n}))\r\nLUA\r\n```\r\n\r\nExpected output on `master`:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n{ 1, 9, 1, 9 }\r\n```\r\n\r\nExpected output with this patch:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n{ 1, 6, 7, 9 }\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe functional reproduction can be run with:\r\n\r\n```sh\r\nmake functionaltest TEST_FILE=test/functional/plugin/lsp/folding_range_spec.lua\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\nCount the ranges ending on each row and emit the lowest ending fold level. Add a functional test covering adjacent outer ranges with nested ranges.\r\n\r\n## AI disclosure\r\n\r\nOpenAI Codex assisted with analyzing the folding behavior, preparing the implementation and tests. The commit includes the `AI-assisted: Codex` trailer.",
